msgid "**Seamless NGINX Reload:** Reloads the NGINX service automatically after successful renewals."
msgstr "**Суусуз NGINX кайра жүктөө:** NGINX кызматын ийгиликтүү жаңылоодон кийин автоматтык түрдө кайра жүктөйт."

msgid "**Search allocation precheck:** [search_allocation_precheck.sh](files/shell/search_allocation_precheck.sh) creates a one-shard probe index named after the container and fails with the answer of `_cluster/allocation/explain`, which names the disk watermark when the data disk is too full, unless Elasticsearch allocates it. `init` runs it in front of `zammad-init`. In compose, the one-shot `precheck` service runs it first and `init` depends on that service, so `compose up` stops on a refusal. Swarm renders no `precheck` service, because `depends_on` does not gate `init` there. Instead the deploy waits until the newest `init` task is `Complete`: a refusing Elasticsearch leaves it `Failed`, the wait stops the deploy, and `docker service logs` of `init` carries the explanation. An `init` the stack deploy did not restart is already `Complete` and passes at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Search allocation precheck:** `search_allocation_precheck.sh <files/shell/search_allocation_precheck.sh>`__ creates a one-shard probe index named after the container and fails with the answer of ``_cluster/allocation/explain``, which names the disk watermark when the data disk is too full, unless Elasticsearch allocates it. ``init`` runs it in front of ``zammad-init``. In compose, the one-shot ``precheck`` service runs it first and ``init`` depends on that service, so ``compose up`` stops on a refusal. Swarm renders no ``precheck`` service, because ``depends_on`` does not gate ``init`` there. Instead the deploy waits until the newest ``init`` task is ``Complete``: a refusing Elasticsearch leaves it ``Failed``, the wait stops the deploy, and ``docker service logs`` of ``init`` carries the explanation. An ``init`` the stack deploy did not restart is already ``Complete`` and passes at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Search index per Elasticsearch major:** The index lives in a directory named after the Elasticsearch major inside the ``zammad_search`` volume. A new major starts on an empty directory and ``zammad-init`` rebuilds the index from the database on the same deploy."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Search index per Elasticsearch major:** The index lives in a directory named after the Elasticsearch major inside the `zammad_search` volume. A new major starts on an empty directory and `zammad-init` rebuilds the index from the database on the same deploy."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Search, Favourites and Recently Used**: a search field in the header finds every application by title, description, tags and folder names; pinned and recently opened applications sit at the top level."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Search.** It searches applications only, by title, description, tags and folder names, across all folders, live while typing, and shows its hits in a picker with icon, title and folder path."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Second login:** behind the oauth2 gate the render queue still asks for its own login. It accepts the inventory password of the account; a password changed later in Keycloak does not reach it."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Secret hygiene:** Every stored audit payload is passed through ``utils.agents.redact``, so a token, key or password spooled into a note never reaches the chain; the Keycloak and LDAP admin credentials are read from the environment and never logged."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Secret hygiene:** Every stored audit payload is passed through `utils.agents.redact`, so a token, key or password spooled into a note never reaches the chain; the Keycloak and LDAP admin credentials are read from the environment and never logged."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Secret path:** The client secret reaches the container base64-encoded on the task's stdin and travels on as an environment variable named by `container exec -e`, so it is never on an argv. Proxmox VE stores it as `client-key` in `/etc/pve/domains.cfg`, where its own `pveum realm add` keeps it."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Secret path:** The client secret reaches the container base64-encoded on the task’s stdin and travels on as an environment variable named by ``container exec -e``, so it is never on an argv. Proxmox VE stores it as ``client-key`` in ``/etc/pve/domains.cfg``, where its own ``pveum realm add`` keeps it."
msgstr ""

# *web-app-openbao* ролдук жайгаштыруу OpenBao жерде *Openbao.<domain>*Ал
# колдонмонун күбөлүктөрүн сактайт, API ачкычтар жана машинанын
# идентификациясы. Адамдар кол коюшат KeycloakLDAP аркылуу же OpenLDAP
# жайгаштырылган. Алардын өздөрүнүн *администратор администратор
# администратор*, *оператордун оператору* же же *окурман* Топ өздөрү эмнеге
# жете аларын чечет жана ошол топтордун бирисиз кол коюу демейки саясатты гана
# берет. Кызматтар жана автоматташтыруу өзүлөрүнүн AppRole идентификацияларын
# колдонушат, алардын күбөлүктөрү ар бир жайгаштырууда өзгөрөт жана биринчи
# жайгаштыруудан кийин эч кандай тамыр белгиси сакталбайт. Токен дүкөнүндө
# сакталган калыбына келтирүү ачкычы, эгерде ал күбөлүктөр жоголсо,
# кийинчерээк жайгаштырууга мүмкүндүк берет. Статикалык пломба ар бир кайра
# башталгандан кийин түйүндү өз алдынча бошотот. Ички PKI күйгүзүлүшү мүмкүн,
# Prometheus Бул түйүн жабык же жеткиликтүү эмес болгондо эскертүү берет. Мөөр
# ачкычы шифрленген инвентаризацияда гана жашайт, ал эми дүкөндүн камдык
# көчүрмөсүн алсыз окууга болбойт, ошондуктан инвентаризацияны көлөм менен
# кошо камдыкта сактаңыз. караңыз
# [web-app-openbao](roles/web-app-openbao/README.md)....
msgid "**Secrets management with OpenBao.** The new *web-app-openbao* role deploys OpenBao at *openbao.<domain>*. It stores application credentials, API keys and machine identities. People sign in through Keycloak, or through LDAP when OpenLDAP is deployed. Their *administrator*, *operator* or *reader* group decides what they can reach, and signing in without one of those groups grants only the default policy. Services and automation use their own AppRole identities, whose credentials change on every deploy, and no root token is kept after the first deploy. A recovery key kept in the token store lets a later deploy get back in if those credentials are lost. A static seal unseals the node on its own after every restart. An internal PKI can be switched on, and Prometheus alerts when the node is sealed or unreachable. The seal key lives only in the encrypted inventory, and a backup of the store cannot be read without it, so back up the inventory together with the volume. See [web-app-openbao](roles/web-app-openbao/README.md)."
msgstr ""

# translated-by: libretranslate
